\subsection{Start of a round: Carrying results over from earlier rounds}
\label{sec:carryover}

Every round of a course has some students who took it before.
They only do the assignments they have not passed, and their earlier
results stay in the Canvas room of the round they took.
Everything that reads one room then gets them wrong:
[[canvaslms results]] does not see the module complete, and a list of
missing assignments names what the student passed a year ago.
So at the start of a round we copy the passed results into the new room.

This script decides what to copy, but it does not copy anything.
It prints [[canvaslms grade]] commands for us to read, and we run them:
\begin{minted}{bash}
carryover.sh tilkry27 > carryover-commands.sh
less carryover-commands.sh
bash carryover-commands.sh
\end{minted}
The commands write grades to Canvas, which is all the more reason to read
them first---and to run them soon after.
They are made from what the new room held when we asked, and a submission
that is graded or handed in after that is overwritten all the same.
What cannot be carried over is printed as comments, starting with [[#]],
so the output can go to the shell as it is.

When a student writes to say they passed something in an earlier round,
we need the commands for that one student only:
\begin{minted}{bash}
carryover.sh -u 'student@kth.se' tilkry27
\end{minted}
It prints only what concerns that student, and it is faster than a run
for the whole round, though not as much faster as one would like
(\cref{sec:carryover-fetch}).

Like [[ungraded.sh]] in \cref{sec:cron-ungraded}, this script is shipped
with [[canvaslms]], and that section shows where it is installed.
And like that script, it keeps what is personal out of itself:
how a course's assignments were renamed from one round to the next is
known only to its teachers, so the rules for that live in a file of
their own (\cref{sec:carryover-rules}).


\subsubsection{The script as a whole}

The script has three parts, and we present them from the inside out.
\begin{description}
\item[The decision] [[carryover_plan]] reads files that hold what
  [[canvaslms]] printed about the new room and the earlier rounds, and
  prints the commands.
  It reads nothing from Canvas, so we can test it on rounds we make up.
\item[The Canvas side] [[carryover]] finds the new room and the earlier
  rounds of its course, fetches them into a temporary directory, and
  hands that directory to [[carryover_plan]].
\item[The command line] [[carryover_main]] reads the options and calls
  [[carryover]].
\end{description}
The script runs [[carryover_main]] only when it is run as a program.
When it is sourced, it just defines the functions, so that a test---ours
below, or that of a program that keeps its own rules---can call
[[carryover_plan]] on files of its own.
<<[[carryover.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash
# carryover.sh - print canvaslms commands that carry returning students'
# passes over from the course's earlier rounds into a new round
#
# Usage: carryover.sh [-u student] [-r rules] [-m message] course
#
# It only prints commands, and never writes to Canvas itself: read them,
# then run them.  A rule line is a course code, spaces or tabs and a regex
# matching the names that one assignment has had.  Rules come from -r and
# then from ${CANVASLMS_CARRYOVER_RULES:-~/.config/canvaslms/carryover.rules}.
#
# Sourced, it only defines its functions, among them
#   carryover_plan DIR CANVAS_ID COURSE_CODE [MESSAGE]
# which decides on files instead of asking Canvas.

<<constants>>
<<functions>>

if [[ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" == "$0" ]]; then
  carryover_main "$@"
fi
@

