\subsubsection{Rules for renamed assignments}
\label{sec:carryover-rules}

An assignment is the same in two rounds when it has the same name.
Often it has not:
of the 48 assignments of one round of a cryptography course, only 30 had
a name from the round before.
Some were new, but many were only renamed, and how varies with the
course:
\begin{itemize}
\item the date and the teachers of a seminar are in its name, and the
  word for mandatory comes and goes:
  \enquote{LIVE (MANDATORY) Seminar 4/2: usability (\ldots)} one year,
  \enquote{MANDATORY Seminar (31/1): usability (\ldots) ON CAMPUS}
  another;
\item one round adds \enquote{(obligatorisk)} to every mandatory
  assignment;
\item a final seminar is dated: \enquote{The final seminar on 27/5 at
  14:15}.
\end{itemize}
What stays is a core---\enquote{: usability}, \enquote{Excel labb 2},
\enquote{The final seminar on}---and a regex can say what that core is.
So a rule is a course code and a regex, separated by spaces or tabs:
every name that the regex matches is the same assignment.
\Cref{fig:carryover-rules} shows a few.
\begin{figure}
\begin{minted}{text}
# course  names of one assignment
DD2520  Seminar.*: usability|^Usability seminar$
DD2520  ^INL1Written( Final| [0-9/-]+)?$
DD1317  ^Excel labb 2( \(obligatorisk\))?$
DA2215  ^The final seminar on
\end{minted}
\caption{A rules file.
  Empty lines and lines starting with [[#]] are ignored; a regex may hold
  spaces.}
\label{fig:carryover-rules}
\end{figure}
The seminar shows why a rule names the core and not the variation:
[[Seminar.*: usability]] matches every year's seminar but not the
\enquote{Preparation for usability seminar}, which is an assignment of
its own.
The written assignment of the second rule became \enquote{INL1Written
Final} when a peer review was added, \enquote{INL1Written Peer Review},
which the rule must not match.

A rule is used like this.
A name that the new room has, once, is the same assignment there, rules
or not.
Otherwise we take the rules for the course in order, and the first one
that matches the old name decides:
if it matches exactly one name in the new room, that is the assignment;
if it matches several, we cannot tell which, and report it.
A rule that matches no name in the room decides nothing, and we try the
next---so a rule for one run may be narrower than our usual one without
hiding it.
A name that the new room has twice is no assignment to copy to either:
the command names an assignment by a regex on its name, and that would
grade both.
A room can have such names, for instance when the same assignments are
imported into it twice.

The rules are the teachers' knowledge of their courses, so the script
has none of its own: a rule built into a script shipped with
[[canvaslms]] would be someone else's course, and it would change only
with a release.
They come from two files, which are read in this order:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the file given with [[-r]], for one run;
\item our own file, [[carryover.rules]] in [[~/.config/canvaslms]] (or
  in [[canvaslms]] under [[XDG_CONFIG_HOME]]), where [[canvaslms]] keeps
  its configuration on Linux, or the file that
  [[CANVASLMS_CARRYOVER_RULES]] names.
\end{enumerate}
Either may be missing; without rules, an assignment is carried over only
to the same name.
<<functions>>=
carryover_rules_path() {
  echo "${CANVASLMS_CARRYOVER_RULES:-${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/canvaslms/carryover.rules}"
}

carryover_rules() {
  local given="$1" own
  own=$(carryover_rules_path)
  if [[ -n "${given}" ]]; then
    cat "${given}" || return 1
    echo
  fi
  if [[ -e "${own}" ]]; then
    cat "${own}" || return 1
    echo
  fi
}
@ The [[echo]] after each file is for a last line without its newline,
which would otherwise run into the first rule of the next file and take
it along.
A file given with [[-r]] must be there, as must our own file when it
exists but cannot be read: either is a mistake, and a silent run without
the rules would carry less than we think.


\subsubsection{Deciding what to carry over}
\label{sec:carryover-plan}

[[carryover_plan]] reads six files from a directory, each holding what one
[[canvaslms]] command printed, as it printed it:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[rules]] ] the rules, from the files above;
\item[ [[users]] ] [[canvaslms users -s]] of the new room;
\item[ [[assignments]] ] [[canvaslms assignments list]] of the new room;
\item[ [[submissions]] ] [[canvaslms submissions list -l]] of the new room;
\item[ [[rounds]] ] [[canvaslms submissions list -l]] of the earlier rounds;
\item[ [[room]] ] the new room's Canvas course code, such as
  \enquote{DD2520 VT27 (tilkry27)}.
\end{description}
The other arguments are the new room's Canvas ID, which the commands name
it by, and its course code, which selects the rules.
A student is their login ID, the second column of [[canvaslms users]]
and, with [[-l]], the third of [[canvaslms submissions list]].
We do not go by the name, since two students can share one.
A student without a login ID is printed with an empty one by
[[canvaslms users]] but with another identifier by
[[canvaslms submissions list]], so such a student's passes are left out,
without a report.
The other columns of [[submissions list]] are the room's Canvas course
code, the assignment, the grade and the dates of submission and grading;
a submission that nobody has graded has an empty grade, and a date that
is not there is [[N/A]].

All of the deciding is one [[awk]] program:
<<functions>>=
carryover_plan() {
  local data="$1" id="$2" code="$3"
  local message="${4:-${CARRYOVER_MESSAGE}}"
  CARRYOVER_MESSAGE="${message}" \
  awk -F '\t' -v OFS='\t' -v q="'" -v id="${id}" -v code="${code}" \
      -v data="${data}" '
    <<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>
    BEGIN {
      <<rank the passes>>
      getline room < (data "/room")
      message = ENVIRON["CARRYOVER_MESSAGE"]
    }
    { sub(/\r$/, "") }
    <<read the rules>>
    { unquote() }
    <<read the new room>>
    <<keep each student's best pass of each assignment>>
    END {
      <<print the commands that carry the grades over>>
      <<print what could not be carried over>>
    }' "${data}/rules" "${data}/users" "${data}/assignments" \
       "${data}/submissions" "${data}/rounds" \
  | sort -t $'\t' -k 1,1n -k 2,2nr -k 3 | cut -f 3-
  carryover_status "${PIPESTATUS[@]}"
}

carryover_status() {
  local status
  for status in "$@"; do
    [[ "${status}" -eq 0 ]] || return "${status}"
  done
}
<<constants>>=
CARRYOVER_MESSAGE="Result carried over from"
@ The program prints each line behind two numbers, which the [[sort]]
orders the lines by: what kind of line it is, the commands first and the
comments after, and then a count, the largest first; the lines that tie
on both are sorted as text.
The function's status is that of the first program in the pipeline that
failed, not that of [[cut]], which is the status of a pipeline:
a program that sources the script and gives [[carryover_plan]] files of
its own learns that way when [[awk]] could not read one.

The message is what the students see as a comment on the grade, with the
round's Canvas course code after it; it can be changed with [[-m]], for a
course given in Swedish.
The program gets it from its environment and not with [[-v]], which would
read a backslash in it as the start of an escape.

The first rule strips the carriage return from every line, since
[[canvaslms]] writes its rows with [[csv.writer]], which ends them in
one.
It matters for the last column only, which is where the date of grading
is, and there it matters a lot:
[[N/A]] with a carriage return is not [[N/A]], and it would sort after
every date.

The files come in a fixed order, and we tell them apart by name.
Each of the rules below ends in [[next]], so only the last file, the
other rounds', reaches the rules after them.
Counting files with [[FNR == 1]] would have been shorter than going by
their names, but an empty file has no first line: a room where nothing is
graded yet would have been taken for the other rounds.
A rule line is split by hand, since its separator is spaces as well as
tabs, and its regex may contain spaces.
A line with a course code and only blanks after it would give the empty
regex, which matches every name and would make every old name ambiguous,
so it is no rule.
<<read the rules>>=
FILENAME == data "/rules" {
  if ($0 ~ /^[ \t]*(#|$)/) next
  if (match($0, /^[^ \t]+[ \t]+/) && substr($0, 1, RLENGTH) ~ "^" code "[ \t]") {
    pattern = substr($0, RLENGTH + 1)
    sub(/[ \t]+$/, "", pattern)
    if (pattern != "") rule[++rules] = pattern
  }
  next
}
@ The other files are what [[canvaslms]] printed, and [[csv.writer]]
puts a field in double quotes when it contains one, doubling the quotes
inside: an assignment named [[Lab "AES"]] is printed as
[["Lab ""AES"""]].
We undo that in every field, so that a name is the name that
[[canvaslms grade]] will match the command's regex against.
Changing a field makes [[awk]] join the line again with [[OFS]], which is
why the program sets it to a tab.
<<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>=
function unquote(   i, f) {
  for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) {
    f = $i
    if (f ~ /^".*"$/) {
      f = substr(f, 2, length(f) - 2)
      gsub(/""/, "\"", f)
      $i = f
    }
  }
}
<<read the new room>>=
FILENAME == data "/users" { student[$2] = 1; next }
FILENAME == data "/assignments" { copies[$3]++; next }
FILENAME == data "/submissions" {
  if ($4 != "" || $5 != "N/A") taken[$2, $3] = 1
  if (class($4) != "") scale[$2] = class($4)
  next
}
@ What is graded or handed in in the new room already, we leave alone,
even a fail:
whoever graded it saw something we do not, and a student who hands in
again wants it graded.
The grades that are there also tell us the assignment's scale, which we
come back to below.

\paragraph{Made-up rounds to test with}

Before we go into the decisions, we make up a new round of a course and
two earlier ones to try them on, with a student for each decision.
In the new room, we give each assignment that a mistake in a regex could
hit instead a neighbour that differs by little: the same name with
something before or after it, the parentheses without their meaning, a
dot where any character would do.
