@ The output is meant for the shell, so the tests give it to one.
In place of [[canvaslms]] we define a function that prints its arguments,
run each command line, and parse the arguments with the real parser of
[[canvaslms grade]].
Its regexes we then match as [[canvaslms]] would, with Python's [[re]],
against all the names in the room, neighbours and copies counted, and all
the students, without regard to case.
Among the students we count one without a login ID, whom [[canvaslms]]
matches as an empty string, and the student who is not in the new room.
<<test functions>>=
def grade_parser():
  parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
  canvaslms.cli.grade.add_command(parser.add_subparsers(dest="command"))
  return parser

def commands(output):
  """Return the arguments of each command in output, as the shell gives them."""
  found = []
  for line in output.splitlines():
    if line.startswith("#"):
      continue
    printed = subprocess.run(
      ["bash", "-c", 'canvaslms() { printf "%s\\0" "$@"; }; ' + line],
      capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout
    found.append(printed.split("\0")[:-1])
  return found

MESSAGE = "Result carried over from"

def graded(output, message=MESSAGE):
  """Return, per student, what the commands give them: assignment -> grade.

  An assignment is a name if the command names exactly one in the room,
  otherwise the tuple of all the names it matches.  The grade is the pair of
  the grade and the round it comes from.
  """
  everyone = LOGINS + [login("left"), ""]
  result = {}
  for arguments in commands(output):
    args = grade_parser().parse_args(arguments)
    assert args.command == "grade" and args.course == f"^{ROOM_ID}$"
    names = tuple(a for a in ASSIGNMENTS if re.search(args.assignment, a))
    name = names[0] if len(names) == 1 else names
    assert args.message.startswith(message + " ")
    assert args.message.endswith(".")
    round_ = args.message[len(message) + 1:-1]
    for user in everyone:
      if re.search(args.user, user, re.IGNORECASE):
        student = (user.removesuffix("@kth.se") if user.endswith("@kth.se")
                   else user or "(no login ID)")
        result.setdefault(student, {})[name] = (args.grade, round_)
  return result

def reports(output):
  return [line for line in output.splitlines() if line.startswith("#")]
@

\paragraph{Which grades count}

A grade is worth carrying over if it passes.
There are four kinds of them:
\begin{description}
\item[Letters] A to E.
\item[Passes] P, and the P+ that some courses use for a pass with
  distinction.
\item[Completes] \enquote{complete}, the pass of an assignment graded
  complete or incomplete.
\item[Points] above zero, for assignments scored in points whose sum sets
  a grade.
\end{description}
P and \enquote{complete} are both a pass, but they are spellings of
different scales, and we keep them apart for the scale check below rather
than guess whether Canvas would take the one for the other.
Everything else stays: a fail, zero points, an empty grade.
That includes a survey that was handed in for no points: the survey
modules of [[canvaslms]] count it as done for being submitted, but there
is no passing grade to carry over, and it is not reported either.
<<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>=
function class(grade) {
  if (toupper(grade) ~ /^[A-E]$/) return "letter"
  if (toupper(grade) ~ /^(P|P\+)$/) return "pass"
  if (toupper(grade) == "COMPLETE") return "complete"
  if (grade ~ /^[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$/ && grade + 0 > 0) return "points"
  return ""
}
@ Of several passes of the same assignment, from different rounds or a
redone one, we take the best of its kind: the higher letter, the more
points, and P+ before P; one complete is as good as another.
<<rank the passes>>=
split("E D C B A", letters, " ")
for (i = 1; i <= 5; i++) letter_value[letters[i]] = i
<<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>=
function value(grade, kind) {
  if (kind == "letter") return letter_value[toupper(grade)]
  if (kind == "pass") return (toupper(grade) == "P+" ? 2 : 1)
  if (kind == "complete") return 1
  return grade + 0
}
@ Between equal passes, we take the one graded last.
[[canvaslms]] prints the dates as [[YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm]], which sort as
strings, but it prints [[N/A]] when there is none, and that would sort
after every date; so we read it as no date.

Passes of different kinds do not compare, and they do not mix either:
a letter written into an assignment graded pass or fail is not a grade it
can have.
So when the new room has graded the assignment already, its grades tell
us the kind, and a pass of another kind is reported rather than carried;
when it has not, a student whose passes are of different kinds is
reported too.
At the start of a round, though, nothing is graded yet, and we cannot
tell the kind: [[canvaslms assignments list]] does not show how an
assignment is graded.
A pass of one kind then goes where it went before, which is right as long
as the assignment is graded as it was; if its scale has changed, it is
the commands for it that we must read with care.

\paragraph{The best pass}

With that, the other rounds' file is read line by line.
<<keep each student's best pass of each assignment>>=
{
  <<skip what we shouldn't carry over>>
  pair = name SUBSEP $3
  if (!((pair, kind) in best)) kinds[pair]++
  key = pair SUBSEP kind
  date = ($6 == "N/A" ? "" : $6)
  v = value($4, kind)
  if (!(key in best) || v > best_value[key] \
      || (v == best_value[key] && date > best_date[key])) {
    best[key] = $4; best_value[key] = v; best_date[key] = date
    from[key] = $1
  }
}
@ We skip the lines of students who are not in the new room, and the
grades that do not pass.
If the old name gives no assignment in the new room, we note the student
under the old name, to report it later.
<<skip what we shouldn't carry over>>=
if (!($3 in student)) next
kind = class($4)
if (kind == "") next
name = assignment_in_room($2)
if (name == "") { missed[$2, $3] = 1; next }
if ((name, $3) in taken) { kept[name, $3] = 1; next }
@ Let's verify the choice of grades on the students who have more than
one pass, or none.
[[again]] passed the lab twice (P+, then P), the written assignment twice
(A, then B), a quiz twice, the second time without a date, and an
optional lab twice (10 points, then 9.5, which comes first as text);
[[weak]] passed the lab with an E and a quiz twice with a P, the second
time later; [[failed]] failed the lab and got no
points in the quiz; and [[left]] passed the lab but is not in the new
room.
<<test functions>>=
def test_the_best_pass_of_each_kind_is_carried(plan):
  carried = graded(plan())
  assert carried["again"] == {
    "LAB1 Implement AES": ("P+", R25),
    "INL1Written Final": ("A", R25),
    "INL1Quiz Ciphers": ("P", R25),
    "Optional: Side channels (C, B, A)": ("10", R25),
  }
  assert carried["weak"] == {"LAB1 Implement AES": ("E", R26),
                            "Quiz 1.0": ("P", R26)}

def test_fails_and_students_who_left_get_nothing(plan):
  carried = graded(plan())
  assert "failed" not in carried
  assert "left" not in carried
@

\paragraph{Which assignment an old name is}

Which assignment an old name is in the new room is what the rules in
\cref{sec:carryover-rules} decide.
We remember each answer, as an old name comes once for every student of
its round; and why there was none, for the report.
<<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>=
function assignment_in_room(old,   i, a, n, found, names) {
  if (old in resolved) return resolved[old]
  if (old in copies) return resolved[old] = once(old, old)
  for (i = 1; i <= rules; i++) {
    if (old !~ rule[i]) continue
    n = 0
    for (a in copies)
      if (a ~ rule[i]) names[++n] = found = a
    if (n == 1) return resolved[old] = once(old, found)
    if (n > 1) {
      trouble[old] = "which matches several in " room ": " listed(names, n)
      return resolved[old] = ""
    }
  }
  trouble[old] = "which " room " does not have"
  return resolved[old] = ""
}
function once(old, a) {
  if (copies[a] == 1) return a
  trouble[old] = "but " room " has " shown(a) " twice"
  return ""
}
@ The names are listed in order, so that the report is the same from one
run to the next; [[for (a in copies)]] goes through them in no order that
[[awk]] promises.
<<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>=
function listed(names, n,   i, s) {
  sorted(names, n)
  for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) s = s (i > 1 ? ", " : "") shown(names[i])
  return s
}
function sorted(a, n,   i, j, t) {
  for (i = 2; i <= n; i++)
    for (j = i; j > 1 && a[j - 1] > a[j]; j--) {
      t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j - 1]; a[j - 1] = t
    }
  return n
}
@ Now let's verify the rules on [[returning]], who passed eight
assignments in the first earlier round:
the lab, which is in the new room, ungraded;
the written assignment, renamed, and the usability seminar, renamed in a
way that the first of the test's rules for it does not match;
the seminar, the quiz and a lab, whose names need escaping, the lab's
with double quotes, which [[csv.writer]] quotes;
a quiz that has become two, an assignment that the new room has twice,
and one that it does not have.
The test's rules also have one for another course, whose
\enquote{Peer Review} would have matched an assignment in the room, one
for a course whose code starts with this one's, a line with nothing
after its course code, and one with blanks after its regex.
[[second]] passed the lab in the same round as [[returning]].
<<test functions>>=
def test_rules_map_renamed_assignments(plan):
  carried = graded(plan())
  assert carried["returning"] == {
    "LAB1 Implement AES": ("P", R25),
    "INL1Written Final": ("B", R25),
    "Seminar (Bob's part)": ("complete", R25),
    "Quiz 1.0": ("P", R25),
    "MANDATORY Seminar (29, 30/1): usability ON CAMPUS": ("P", R25),
    'Lab "AES"': ("P", R25),
  }
  assert carried["second"] == {"LAB1 Implement AES": ("P", R25)}
@

\paragraph{The commands}

For each student and assignment we have now the best pass of each kind.
If the room has graded the assignment, its kind is the one to carry;
otherwise the student's passes must all be of one kind.
<<print the commands that carry the grades over>>=
for (key in best) {
  split(key, part, SUBSEP)
  pair = part[1] SUBSEP part[2]
  if (part[1] in scale) {
    if (part[3] != scale[part[1]]) {
      if (!((pair, scale[part[1]]) in best)) misfit[pair] = 1
      continue
    }
  } else if (kinds[pair] > 1) {
    mixed[pair] = 1
    continue
  }
  cmd = part[1] SUBSEP best[key] SUBSEP from[key]
  if (cmd in users) users[cmd] = users[cmd] SUBSEP part[2]
  else users[cmd] = part[2]
}
@ The students who get the same grade from the same round in the same
assignment can share a command, since [[canvaslms grade]] grades every
student its [[-u]] regex matches.
That makes far fewer calls to Canvas than one per student and assignment.
We sort the students of a command, so that the output is the same from
one run to the next; the regex would not mind.
The [[if]] could have been a conditional expression, but not every
[[awk]] evaluates [[cmd in users]] before the assignment creates the
element it tests for: [[mawk]] and BusyBox's [[awk]] do not, and the
regex would start with an empty alternative, which matches every student
without a login ID.
<<print the commands that carry the grades over>>=
for (cmd in users) {
  split(cmd, part, SUBSEP)
  n = sorted(who, split(users[cmd], who, SUBSEP))
  logins = regex(who[1])
  for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) logins = logins "|" regex(who[i])
  printf "1\t0\tcanvaslms grade -c %s -a %s -g %s -m %s -u %s\n",
    quote("^" id "$"), quote("^" regex(part[1]) "$"), quote(part[2]),
    quote(message " " part[3] "."), quote("^(" logins ")$")
}
@ The names in the commands are regexes, and they are read by the shell
first.
An assignment name may hold anything---[[(]], a dot, an apostrophe---so
we escape what a regex would read as an operator, anchor it, and put it
in single quotes, which leave everything alone but a single quote.
That one we end the quote for, escape and start the quote again.
We do that with [[index]] and [[substr]] rather than [[gsub]]: how a
backslash in the replacement of [[gsub]] is read differs between the
[[awk]]s, and BusyBox's drops the one we need, which leaves the quote
open and garbles every command after it.
The program itself stands in single quotes in the script, so it cannot
contain one; it gets it as the variable [[q]] instead.
<<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>=
function regex(s) {
  gsub(/[][\\.^$*+?(){}|\/]/, "\\\\&", s)
  return s
}
function quote(s,   out, i) {
  out = ""
  while ((i = index(s, q)) > 0) {
    out = out substr(s, 1, i - 1) q "\\" q q
    s = substr(s, i + 1)
  }
  return q out s q
}
@ The comments are never run, so they show the names as they are.
<<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>=
function shown(s) {
  return q s q
}
@ Let's verify that the commands do what we want, and only that.
Each must name exactly one assignment in the room, and grade exactly the
students we meant: [[graded]] would otherwise give a tuple of names, or a
neighbour, a student not in the room or the student without a login ID.
The students who share a grade share one command.
<<test functions>>=
def test_each_command_grades_exactly_what_it_should(plan):
  carried = graded(plan())
  assert sorted(carried) == ["again", "both", "returning", "second",
                             "weak"]
  assert all(isinstance(name, str)
             for grades in carried.values() for name in grades)

def test_students_with_the_same_grade_share_a_command(plan):
  lab = [args for args in commands(plan())
         if args[args.index("-a") + 1] == "^LAB1 Implement AES$"
         and args[args.index("-g") + 1] == "P"]
  assert [args[args.index("-u") + 1] for args in lab] == [
    r"^(returning@kth\.se|second@kth\.se)$"]
@ The message reaches the command as it was given, a backslash included.
<<test functions>>=
def test_the_message_is_passed_on_untouched(plan):
  message = r"Resultat från tidigare (se \n)"
  carried = graded(plan(message), message)
  assert carried["weak"]["LAB1 Implement AES"] == ("E", R26)
@

\paragraph{What could not be carried over}

What we cannot carry over, we report as comments.
A pass in an assignment that we found no place for in the new room is
what a rule is for, so we list those names with the number of students
who passed them; the most common ones first, as they are the ones most
worth a rule.
<<print what could not be carried over>>=
for (key in missed) {
  split(key, part, SUBSEP)
  passed[part[1]]++
}
for (old in passed)
  printf "2\t%d\t# %d passed %s, %s\n",
    passed[old], passed[old], shown(old), trouble[old]
@ Then the passes of the wrong kind, per assignment, and what we left
alone.
<<print what could not be carried over>>=
for (pair in misfit) { split(pair, part, SUBSEP); wrong[part[1]]++ }
for (a in wrong)
  printf "3\t%d\t# %d not carried into %s: %s grades it otherwise\n",
    wrong[a], wrong[a], shown(a), room
for (pair in mixed) { split(pair, part, SUBSEP); unsure[part[1]]++ }
for (a in unsure)
  printf "3\t%d\t# %d not carried into %s: passes of different kinds\n",
    unsure[a], unsure[a], shown(a)
for (pair in kept) left_alone++
if (left_alone)
  printf "4\t0\t# %d left alone in %s, already graded or handed in\n",
    left_alone, room
@ Let's verify the report, and that a plan whose files cannot be read
says so with its status, not only on standard error.
Three of [[returning]]'s passes have no place: the quiz that became
several, the assignment the room has twice, which [[second]] passed too,
and the one it does not have.
The names that more students passed come first.
[[lettered]] got a B in the seminar, which the new room grades complete
or not, and [[spelled]] a P; [[both]] got a complete and a B, so the
complete is carried and nothing reported; [[mixed]] got P and then A in the written assignment, which the
new room has not graded yet.
[[graded]] passed the lab, which is graded, F, in the new room, and
[[submitted]] has handed it in again there; both are left alone.
<<test functions>>=
def test_what_is_not_carried_is_reported(plan):
