\subsubsection{The other rounds}

Now to the Canvas side.
A course has a Canvas room for each round, and the rooms share nothing
reliable but the course code at the start of their Canvas course code:
the rooms of one course include [[DD1301 HT20 (50850)]], [[DD1301HT21]]
and [[DD1301 HT23 (datintro23p2)]].
So we look up the room that [[course]] names and take its course code:
two letters and three digits, then a digit or a letter, as in
[[DA150X]].
<<constants>>=
KTH_COURSE_CODE="[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{3}[0-9A-Z]"
@ The grades will be written to the room that [[course]] matches, so it
must match exactly one.
We keep the room's Canvas ID, which the commands will name it by: a regex
on the name, such as [[tilkry27]], is easy to get wrong in a way that
matches another room too.
<<find the room of [[course]] and its other rounds>>=
if ! room=$(canvaslms courses -a -i "${course}"); then
  echo "${0##*/}: canvaslms courses -a -i ${course} failed" >&2
  exit 1
fi
room=$(tr -d '\r' <<< "${room}")
if [[ -z "${room}" || $(wc -l <<< "${room}") -ne 1 ]]; then
  echo "${course}: must match exactly one Canvas room" >&2
  exit 1
fi
id=$(cut -f 1 <<< "${room}")
room_name=$(cut -f 2 <<< "${room}")
code=$(grep -oE "^${KTH_COURSE_CODE}" <<< "${room_name}")
if [[ -z "${code}" ]]; then
  echo "${course}: no course code in ${room_name}" >&2
  exit 1
fi
@ The [[tr]] removes the carriage returns of [[csv.writer]], as in
\cref{sec:carryover-plan}; it runs on its own, after [[canvaslms]], so
that a failing [[canvaslms]] is not taken for a course that matches no
room.

Not every room with that code is a round, though.
Each sitting of a computer exam may have a room of its own, such as
\enquote{DD1310 LAB2 [2025-01-07] Examination i datorsal}, some of them
shared by two courses, and those results are reported from there.
A round is a term after the code, [[HT25]] or [[VT26]], with or without a
space between.
Every other round is one to copy from, whatever its dates:
some rooms have none, parallel rounds overlap, and a room of a round that
has not started holds no grades, so it costs nothing to include.
The two digits are written out rather than as [[{2}]], since some
[[mawk]]s do not know interval expressions, and the regex would then
match no room.
<<constants>>=
TERM_OF_ROUND="[HV]T[0-9][0-9]"
<<find the room of [[course]] and its other rounds>>=
if ! rooms=$(canvaslms courses -a -i); then
  echo "${0##*/}: canvaslms courses failed" >&2
  exit 1
fi
rounds=$(tr -d '\r' <<< "${rooms}" \
         | awk -F '\t' -v round="^${code} ?${TERM_OF_ROUND}" \
             -v id="${id}" '$2 ~ round && $1 != id { print $1 }')
if [[ -z "${rounds}" ]]; then
  echo "# ${course}: no other rounds of ${code} in Canvas"
  exit 0
fi
@ Without the [[-a]], [[canvaslms courses]] lists only the current rooms,
and the other rounds are mostly the ones that are over.
The guard for no rounds is not only for the message:
the regex we make of the rounds below would be [[^()$]], and we would
rather not find out what Canvas makes of that.


\subsubsection{What the rounds hold}
\label{sec:carryover-fetch}

From the new room we need its students, its assignments and its
submissions; from the other rounds, their submissions.
We keep the output of each [[canvaslms]] command in a file of its own, as
it is, and that directory is all that [[carryover_plan]] gets.
The [[student]] filter, when there is one, narrows the students of the
new room and the submissions of every room to the students it matches,
as the positional regex of [[canvaslms users]] and the [[-u]] of
[[canvaslms submissions list]].
<<fetch the room and the other rounds into [[data]]>>=
student_regex=()
student_option=()
if [[ -n "${student}" ]]; then
  student_regex=("${student}")
  student_option=(-u "${student}")
fi
carryover_fetch "${data}/users" \
  users -sc "^${id}$" "${student_regex[@]}" || exit 1
carryover_fetch "${data}/assignments" \
  assignments list -c "^${id}$" || exit 1
carryover_fetch "${data}/submissions" \
  submissions list -c "^${id}$" -l "${student_option[@]}" || exit 1
<<fetch the other rounds' submissions>>
echo "${room_name}" > "${data}/room"
@ A fetch that fails must stop us.
Say the new room's submissions do not come: then nothing in it looks
graded, and we would print commands that overwrite what is.
[[canvaslms]] fails also when there is nothing to list---a room without
assignments yet---and then there is nothing to carry over into either.
<<functions>>=
carryover_fetch() {
  local file="$1"
  shift
  if ! canvaslms "$@" > "${file}"; then
    echo "${0##*/}: canvaslms $* failed" >&2
    return 1
  fi
}
@

The other rounds are another matter.
Canvas has a submission for every student and assignment, submitted or
not---a round of 87 students and 39 assignments has 3393---so without a
filter the rounds' file holds every student's grade in every assignment
of those rounds.
Reading them all takes a while: for tilkry26, with its earlier rounds,
a run took over fifteen minutes.

A student filter cuts that less than one would think.
[[canvaslms submissions list]] fetches each assignment's submissions as a
whole and only then picks the students that [[-u]] matches; the filter
narrows which of the cached submissions it checks for freshness, not
what it asks Canvas for when nothing is cached.
Right after the run for the whole round, with everything in the cache,
the same course took under three minutes for one student, and over five
for a filter that matched a part of the class.
Without the cache, that filter had not finished after fifteen minutes.

But then it is normal that the earlier rounds hold nothing: the student
may have been in none of them.
[[canvaslms]] exits with status~1 when it finds nothing to list, but it
does so also when it fails, so the status cannot tell the two apart.
What it prints can: when nothing is found, the message ends in
\enquote{found matching the criteria}, as for the users or the
submissions, while a failure prints something else, a traceback for
instance.
So we keep what this one fetch prints on standard error, and an empty
earlier round is a comment and a normal end.
It need not be a student filter that makes them empty, so we do not ask
whether there is one: the rounds having nothing to carry is an answer,
not an error.
Anything else is a failure, and we show what [[canvaslms]] said.
<<fetch the other rounds' submissions>>=
if ! canvaslms submissions list \
       -c "^($(paste -sd '|' <<< "${rounds}"))$" -l \
       "${student_option[@]}" > "${data}/rounds" 2> "${data}/errors"; then
  if grep -q "found matching the criteria" "${data}/errors"; then
    echo "# ${student:-${course}}: nothing in the other rounds of ${code}"
    exit 0
  fi
  cat "${data}/errors" >&2
  echo "${0##*/}: canvaslms submissions list of the other rounds failed" >&2
  exit 1
fi
cat "${data}/errors" >&2
@ The warnings of a fetch that worked, such as a note that a listing was
fetched again, still reach us on standard error.
The new room's fetches get no such leniency: an empty new room is no room
to carry into, and its submissions must be there for us to know what not
to overwrite.

The test of the script depends on that message, so we check it against
the real [[canvaslms]], with HTTP stubbed by [[responses]] as in the
tests of the command line in [[cli.nw]]: a recording of Canvas cannot
contain a student who is not there.
The course has an assignment but no students, and a course without
assignments has no submissions either.
[[canvaslms]] prints its errors through [[logging]], which
[[basicConfig]] sends to standard error; under pytest, which installs
handlers of its own first, we read them from [[caplog]] instead.
<<test functions>>=
CANVAS_HOST = "https://canvas.example"
CANVAS_API = f"{CANVAS_HOST}/api/v1"
OLD_COURSE = {"id": 1, "name": "Old course", "course_code": "OLD"}
LAB = {"id": 5, "name": "Lab", "course_id": 1}

@pytest.fixture
def run_cli(monkeypatch, tmp_path, caplog):
  """Return a function running the real canvaslms main, giving its errors."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(canvaslms.cli.cache, "get_cache_path",
                      lambda hostname: str(tmp_path / "cache.enc"))
  monkeypatch.setattr(canvaslms.cli.login, "load_credentials",
                      lambda config: (CANVAS_HOST, "token"))
  monkeypatch.setattr(canvaslms.hacks.canvasapi,
                      "MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES", 0)

  def run(*arguments):
    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, "argv", ["canvaslms", "--config-file",
                                      str(tmp_path / "config.json"),
                                      *arguments])
    caplog.clear()
    with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as exit_info:
      canvaslms.cli.main()
    assert exit_info.value.code == 1
    return "\n".join(record.getMessage() for record in caplog.records
                     if record.levelno >= logging.ERROR)

  return run

@responses.activate
def test_canvaslms_says_when_a_student_has_nothing(run_cli):
  responses.get(f"{CANVAS_API}/courses", json=[OLD_COURSE])
  responses.get(f"{CANVAS_API}/courses/1/assignments", json=[LAB])
  responses.get(f"{CANVAS_API}/courses/1/search_users", json=[])
  responses.get(f"{CANVAS_API}/courses/1/users", json=[])
  errors = run_cli("submissions", "list", "-c", "Old", "-l",
                   "-u", "nobody@kth.se")
  assert "found matching the criteria" in errors

@responses.activate
def test_canvaslms_says_when_a_round_has_nothing(run_cli):
  responses.get(f"{CANVAS_API}/courses", json=[OLD_COURSE])
  responses.get(f"{CANVAS_API}/courses/1/assignments", json=[])
  errors = run_cli("submissions", "list", "-c", "Old", "-l")
  assert "found matching the criteria" in errors
@


\subsubsection{Putting the Canvas side together}

[[carryover]] collects the rules, finds the rooms, fetches them and hands
the files to [[carryover_plan]]:
<<functions>>=
carryover() {
  local course="$1" student="$2" rules="$3" message="$4"
  (
    data=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "${data}"' EXIT
    trap 'exit 130' INT
    carryover_rules "${rules}" > "${data}/rules" || exit 1
    <<find the room of [[course]] and its other rounds>>
    <<fetch the room and the other rounds into [[data]]>>
    carryover_plan "${data}" "${id}" "${code}" "${message}"
  )
}
@ The rules come first, before any call to Canvas: a misspelt [[-r]]
should not cost the twenty minutes of a whole fetch.

The files hold every student's grades, so they must not outlive the run,
not even one we interrupt:
reading the rounds takes minutes, which is time enough to press Ctrl-C.
The body runs in a subshell, whose [[EXIT]] trap removes the files
however it ends, an interrupt included.
An interrupt also reaches the [[canvaslms]] that is running, and
[[bash]], left to itself, waits to see how that ends: a program that
catches the interrupt and exits normally lets the script go on, with half
the data.
The [[INT]] trap ends the subshell instead, with the status that the
interrupt would have given it.
A termination needs no trap of its own: [[bash]] dies of it, without
waiting, and still runs the [[EXIT]] trap on its way out.
A [[TERM]] trap would only make it wait for the program to end first.
The subshell also keeps the variables to itself, and a failure can simply
[[exit]] it.

To test the Canvas side, a stub stands in for [[canvaslms]].
It prints the files of the made-up rounds, as [[canvaslms]] would have
printed them, and notes how it was called.
The list of rooms holds the three rounds, an exam room with the same
course code and a round of another course.
The environment makes it fail, find nothing or hang, for one listing:
[[room]] is the new room's submissions and [[rounds]] the other rounds'.
When it hangs, it catches an interrupt or a termination and exits
normally, the case in which [[bash]] would go on; it can also warn on
standard error and still succeed.
<<test functions>>=
COURSES = [("52629", R25), ("59543", R26), (ROOM_ID, ROOM),
           ("70001", "DD2520 INL1 [2026-03-02] Examination"),
           ("56121", "DD1310 HT25 (CELTE)")]

CANVASLMS_STUB = """#!{python}
import json, os, re, signal, sys, time
args = sys.argv[1:]
with open({log!r}, "a") as log:
  log.write(json.dumps(args) + "\\n")
data = {data!r}
def show(name):
  with open(os.path.join(data, name), "rb") as file:
    sys.stdout.buffer.write(file.read())
listing = (args[0] if args[0] in ("users", "assignments")
           else "room" if args[:2] == ["submissions", "list"]
                          and args[args.index("-c") + 1] == "^{room_id}$"
           else "rounds" if args[0] == "submissions" else args[0])
if os.environ.get("STUB_FAIL") == listing:
  sys.stderr.write("Traceback (most recent call last): ...\\n")
  sys.exit(1)
if os.environ.get("STUB_EMPTY") == listing:
  sys.stderr.write("canvaslms: No submissions found matching the criteria\\n")
  sys.exit(1)
if os.environ.get("STUB_WARN") == listing:
  sys.stderr.write("canvaslms: a warning about the listing\\n")
if os.environ.get("STUB_HANG") == listing:
  signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, lambda *_: sys.exit(0))
  signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda *_: sys.exit(0))
  open(os.path.join(data, "hanging"), "w").close()
  time.sleep(60)
if args[:2] == ["courses", "-a"]:
  with open(os.path.join(data, "courses"), newline="") as file:
    rows = file.readlines()
  if len(args) > 3:
    rows = [row for row in rows if re.search(args[3], row)]
  sys.stdout.write("".join(rows))
elif listing == "room":
  show("submissions")
else:
  show(listing)
"""

@pytest.fixture
def run(tmp_path):
  """Return a function running carryover.sh against the stub canvaslms.

  It returns the result, the recorded calls and what is left in TMPDIR.
  """
  data = tmp_path / "canvas"
  data.mkdir()
  write_canvas_files(data)
  with open(data / "courses", "w", newline="") as file:
    for cid, code in COURSES:
      file.write(f"{cid}\t{code}\t{code}\t\t\r\n")
  bin_dir = tmp_path / "bin"
  bin_dir.mkdir()
  log = tmp_path / "calls.jsonl"
  (bin_dir / "canvaslms").write_text(CANVASLMS_STUB.format(
    python=sys.executable, log=str(log), data=str(data), room_id=ROOM_ID))
  (bin_dir / "canvaslms").chmod(0o755)
  tmp = tmp_path / "tmp"
  tmp.mkdir()

  def run_script(*args, env=None, canvaslms=True):
    run_env = dict(os.environ, TMPDIR=str(tmp),
                   XDG_CONFIG_HOME=str(tmp_path / "xdg"))
    run_env.pop("CANVASLMS_CARRYOVER_RULES", None)
    run_env["PATH"] = (f"{bin_dir}{os.pathsep}{run_env['PATH']}"
                       if canvaslms else "/nonexistent")
    run_env.update(env or {})
    result = subprocess.run([shutil.which("bash"), str(SCRIPT), *args],
                            env=run_env, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                            capture_output=True, text=True)
    calls = ([json.loads(line) for line in log.read_text().splitlines()]
             if log.exists() else [])
    if log.exists():
      log.unlink()
    return result, calls, os.listdir(tmp)

  run_script.data = data
  run_script.tmp = tmp
  run_script.env = lambda: dict(os.environ, TMPDIR=str(tmp),
                                XDG_CONFIG_HOME=str(tmp_path / "xdg"),
                                PATH=f"{bin_dir}{os.pathsep}"
                                     f"{os.environ['PATH']}")
  return run_script
@ Let's verify which rooms the script asks for.
The rounds are the two earlier ones, by their IDs: not the new room, not
the exam room and not the other course; and the new room is named by its
ID too.
We read the calls with the real parsers of [[canvaslms]], since the
options may be bundled, as in [[-sc]].
<<test functions>>=
def option(call, name):
  return call[call.index(name) + 1]

def parsed(calls):
  """Return the calls parsed by the real canvaslms parsers."""
  parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
  subp = parser.add_subparsers(dest="command")
  for module in (canvaslms.cli.courses, canvaslms.cli.users,
                 canvaslms.cli.assignments, canvaslms.cli.submissions):
    module.add_command(subp)
  return [parser.parse_args(call) for call in calls]

def test_the_rounds_are_the_other_terms_of_the_course(run):
  result, calls, _ = run("tilkry27")
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  listings = [args for args in parsed(calls) if args.command != "courses"]
  assert [(args.command, args.course) for args in listings] == [
    ("users", f"^{ROOM_ID}$"), ("assignments", f"^{ROOM_ID}$"),
    ("submissions", f"^{ROOM_ID}$"), ("submissions", "^(52629|59543)$")]
  assert "canvaslms grade -c '^67592$' -a '^LAB1 Implement AES$'" \
    in result.stdout

def test_the_course_must_be_one_room(run):
  result, calls, left = run("tilkry")
  assert result.returncode != 0
  assert "must match exactly one Canvas room" in result.stderr
  assert result.stdout == ""
  assert [call[0] for call in calls] == ["courses"]
  assert left == []

def test_a_course_without_other_rounds_is_no_error(run):
  result, calls, _ = run("CELTE")
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  assert result.stdout == "# CELTE: no other rounds of DD1310 in Canvas\n"
  assert all(call[0] == "courses" for call in calls)
@ The rules come from the two files, in order.
Without rules, the old written assignment of [[returning]] has no place,
since the room has no assignment by that name.
A rule in our own file sends it to the peer review, and one for this run,
given with [[-r]], to the seminar's neighbour without parentheses; neither
file ends its last line with a newline.
<<test functions>>=
def written_assignment(output):
  """Return where returning's B from the old written assignment goes."""
  return [name for name, grade in graded(output).get("returning", {}).items()
          if grade == ("B", R25)]

def test_rules_come_from_the_run_then_our_own_file(run, tmp_path):
  own = tmp_path / "xdg" / "canvaslms" / "carryover.rules"
  own.parent.mkdir(parents=True)
  once = tmp_path / "once.rules"
  once.write_text("DD2520  ^INL1Written$|^Seminar Bob")

  result, _, _ = run("tilkry27")
  assert written_assignment(result.stdout) == []
  assert "passed 'INL1Written', which" in result.stdout

  own.write_text("# my rules\nDD2520  ^INL1Written$|Peer Review")
  result, _, _ = run("tilkry27")
  assert written_assignment(result.stdout) == ["INL1Written Peer Review"]

  result, _, _ = run("-r", str(once), "tilkry27")
  assert written_assignment(result.stdout) == ["Seminar Bob's part"]

def test_the_environment_names_our_own_rules(run, tmp_path):
  mine = tmp_path / "mine.rules"
  mine.write_text("DD2520  ^INL1Written$|Peer Review\n")
  result, _, _ = run("tilkry27",
                     env={"CANVASLMS_CARRYOVER_RULES": str(mine)})
  assert written_assignment(result.stdout) == ["INL1Written Peer Review"]
@ A rules file that is not there must stop the run before it asks Canvas
anything, and so must our own file when it is there but cannot be read.
<<test functions>>=
def test_a_missing_rules_file_stops_before_canvas(run, tmp_path):
  result, calls, left = run("-r", str(tmp_path / "nowhere.rules"),
                            "tilkry27")
  assert result.returncode != 0
  assert "nowhere.rules" in result.stderr
  assert calls == []
  assert left == []

@pytest.mark.skipif(os.geteuid() == 0, reason="root reads any file")
def test_an_unreadable_own_rules_file_stops_too(run, tmp_path):
  own = tmp_path / "xdg" / "canvaslms" / "carryover.rules"
  own.parent.mkdir(parents=True)
  own.write_text("DD2520  ^INL1Written$|Peer Review\n")
  own.chmod(0)
  result, calls, _ = run("tilkry27")
  assert result.returncode != 0
  assert calls == []
@ Then the student filter: it must reach the new room's students and the
submissions of both the new room and the other rounds, and nothing else.
When the other rounds have nothing for the student, that is a comment
and a normal end.
<<test functions>>=
def test_the_student_filter_reaches_every_roster(run):
  result, calls, _ = run("-u", "returning@kth", "tilkry27")
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  users, assignments, room, rounds = [call for call in calls
                                      if call[0] != "courses"]
  assert users[-1] == "returning@kth"
  assert "returning@kth" not in assignments
  assert option(room, "-u") == "returning@kth"
  assert option(rounds, "-u") == "returning@kth"

def test_nothing_in_the_other_rounds_is_no_error(run):
  result, _, left = run("-u", "new@kth.se", "tilkry27",
                        env={"STUB_EMPTY": "rounds"})
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  assert result.stdout == ("# new@kth.se: nothing in the other rounds "
                           "of DD2520\n")
  assert left == []
@ But a failure of the other rounds' fetch stops us, and shows what
[[canvaslms]] said; so does the new room's submissions failing, whether
they fail or come back empty, and so does a failing lookup of the room.
The warnings of a fetch that worked are shown too.
Nothing is printed then, and nothing is left behind.
<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("env", [{"STUB_FAIL": "rounds"},
                                 {"STUB_FAIL": "room"},
                                 {"STUB_EMPTY": "room"},
                                 {"STUB_FAIL": "users"}])
def test_a_failed_fetch_prints_nothing_and_leaves_nothing(run, env):
  result, _, left = run("tilkry27", env=env)
  assert result.returncode != 0
  assert result.stdout == ""
  assert "failed" in result.stderr
  assert left == []

def test_a_failed_rounds_fetch_shows_why(run):
  result, _, _ = run("tilkry27", env={"STUB_FAIL": "rounds"})
  assert "Traceback" in result.stderr

def test_warnings_of_a_fetch_that_worked_get_through(run):
  result, _, _ = run("tilkry27", env={"STUB_WARN": "rounds"})
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  assert "a warning about the listing" in result.stderr

def test_a_failed_room_lookup_says_so(run):
  result, _, left = run("tilkry27", env={"STUB_FAIL": "courses"})
  assert result.returncode != 0
  assert "Traceback" in result.stderr
  assert "canvaslms courses -a -i tilkry27 failed" in result.stderr
  assert "must match exactly one" not in result.stderr
  assert left == []
@ An interrupted run must not leave the files behind either.
We start the script in a session of its own, wait until the stub is
fetching the other rounds, and signal the whole session, as the terminal
does with Ctrl-C.
The run must end without printing a command, with a failing status, and
without its files.
<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("sig", [signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGINT])
def test_an_interrupted_run_leaves_nothing(run, sig):
  process = subprocess.Popen(
    [shutil.which("bash"), str(SCRIPT), "tilkry27"],
    env=dict(run.env(), STUB_HANG="rounds"), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
    stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True,
    start_new_session=True)
  hanging = run.data / "hanging"
  deadline = time.monotonic() + 30
  while not hanging.exists() and time.monotonic() < deadline:
    time.sleep(0.05)
  assert hanging.exists()
  assert os.listdir(run.tmp) != []
  os.killpg(process.pid, sig)
  stdout, _ = process.communicate(timeout=30)
  assert process.returncode != 0
  assert "canvaslms grade" not in stdout
  deadline = time.monotonic() + 10
  while os.listdir(run.tmp) and time.monotonic() < deadline:
    time.sleep(0.05)
  assert os.listdir(run.tmp) == []
@ Finally, the stub accepts any arguments, so we check the recorded calls
against the real parsers of [[canvaslms]].
If one of its options is renamed, this test fails, not the start of a
round.
<<test functions>>=
def test_calls_parse_with_the_real_parsers(run):
  _, calls, _ = run("-u", "returning@kth", "tilkry27")
  calls = parsed(calls)
  assert [args.command for args in calls] == [
    "courses", "courses", "users", "assignments", "submissions",
    "submissions"]
  courses = calls[0]
  assert courses.all and courses.id and courses.regex == "tilkry27"
  assert calls[2].regex == "returning@kth"
  assert all(args.login_id for args in calls[4:])
